  <section class="page cs">
    <p class="kicker col-head">Student Team Daedalus · Curriculum</p>
    <h1 class="col-head">Notes</h1>
    <p class="deck col-prose">The student team I built with, and what I am studying — quartile by quartile.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="page sec" id="daedalus">
    <hr class="sec__rule col-full">
    <p class="sec__reading col-margin">Team · <span class="nc">TU/e</span> · est. 2021</p>
    <h2 class="col-head">Student Team Daedalus</h2>

    <p class="col-prose"><a href="https://www.teamdaedalus.eu/" rel="noopener">Team Daedalus</a> is a multidisciplinary student team at Eindhoven University of Technology, founded in 2021 and based at the TU/e Innovation Space: <span class="nb">39</span> students from more than <span class="nb">15</span> countries building a solar-powered autonomous aircraft for prolonged aerial surveillance, aimed at wildfire detection. Airframes, avionics and power electronics are all designed, manufactured and tested in-house, in iterative prototypes called Lapyx — each one a refinement of the last, each crash folded into the next build.</p>

    <p class="col-prose">The current aircraft, Lapyx 4, has a <span class="nb">4.0 m</span> wingspan, weighs <span class="nb">9.2 kg</span>, carries <span class="nb">225 W</span> of solar cells across the upper wing and three <span class="nb">16 Ah</span> cells in the fuselage, and flies with a fully integrated autopilot. Its maiden flight on 25 February 2026 ran in both manual and autopilot modes, completed a <span class="nb">100 km/h</span> speed test and validated an estimated endurance of about seven hours on battery alone — before a mechanical failure cut the power and ended the flight in a crash. The team rebuilt it over the spring; the second flight was planned for September 2026.</p>

    <p class="col-prose">I joined in January 2026 as an electrical engineer, one of six in the Electrical / Embedded Systems department, and was a member until 14 October 2026. The department owns the aircraft’s electronics — the systems in development include the Daedawatt Ultra MPPT solar charge controller, the DaedaLink avionics bus, the DaedaFind LTE/GNSS tracker and the DaedaBuzz recovery buzzer — and my part of it was PCB design, embedded programming and bench verification.</p>
    <p class="col-prose">The electronics are built alongside seven other departments — mechanical and airframe, avionics, computer and machine vision, business, PR and communication, the board — so a board is designed against an airframe that is being built at the same time, a power budget the solar array sets, and avionics written by someone else. The rebuild after the maiden flight worked the same way: the crash’s lessons had to be carried into every subsystem at once, with the second flight already on the calendar for September.</p>

    <dl class="meta col-full">
      <div class="meta__cell"><dt>Department</dt><dd>Electrical / Embedded Systems</dd></div>
      <div class="meta__cell"><dt>Role</dt><dd>PCB design · embedded programming · bench verification</dd></div>
      <div class="meta__cell"><dt>Member</dt><dd>January – 14 October 2026</dd></div>
      <div class="meta__cell"><dt>Team</dt><dd><a href="https://www.teamdaedalus.eu/" rel="noopener">teamdaedalus.eu <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

